00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:48.380
- Okay, shall I try to get things started

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.700
by initiating this conversation?

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.290
Would that be-
- Absolutely.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:57.290
- Okay, so one of the themes

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.550
in your talk yesterday,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.800
at least as I understood it, Jenann,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.360
was something like the following,

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:07.760
that there are different
ways of approaching the topic

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.250
of causation and causal the
reasoning in philosophy.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:14.660
One way, which is associated with kind

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:17.680
of standard analytic philosophy,

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:19.910
analytic metaphysics, et cetera,

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:24.200
is to ask such questions
as, what is causation?

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:28.000
Or what is included in
our concept of causation?

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:29.980
And then you would do, if
you're doing the latter,

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:32.250
you would do some sort
of conceptual analysis

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:36.150
of our concept of causation.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.740
But there's an alternative way

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:41.580
of approaching the subject of causation.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:46.580
You can ask questions about
the role that causation plays

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:50.667
in our lives, how causal reasoning

00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:53.813
and causal thinking figures in,

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:59.640
so to speak, the psychological
economy of human agents.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.180
You can ask that question.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:05.110
You can ask the question of
what the physical underpinning,

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:06.920
so to speak, of causal thinking are.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:07.920
That it switched the

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:12.140
worldly infrastructure or the
things that have to be true

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:16.763
of the world for causal
reasoning to be successful.

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.500
You emphasized, I think,
both of these in your talk.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:23.000
I would add a third component,

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.010
which is more normative in character.

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:30.010
There are also questions
about what are the strategies

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:34.610
or procedures that make a causal reasoning

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:37.560
or learning about causal relationships

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.960
from certain kinds of data reliable?

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.270
And if you're doing these latter things,

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:46.980
rather than the analytic
metaphysics project,

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.753
things really look rather different.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:54.570
There are different criteria for success.

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:56.430
The whole focus is different.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:01.370
The focus is more, it's
psychological, anthropological.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:03.790
If you're interested in the
normative aspects of things,

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:08.270
there are connections
with work in statistics

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:13.250
on causal reasoning, work
in machine learning and AI

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:19.350
on causal learning, et cetera, et cetera.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:22.770
So I thought maybe one way
we can get started would,

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:25.483
just to talk a little bit about this,

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:30.020
at least as I see it, distinction
between these two ways

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:33.770
of going at this general topic

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:37.210
of trying to understand
causation and causal reasoning.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:39.250
And then there's a further question,

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:40.330
which I think is interesting,

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:44.220
about whether if you do
think that there's some merit

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.420
in this alternative way
of approaching things

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:47.770
that I'm describing,

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:50.780
whether that might be
extended to other notions

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:52.563
that are of philosophical interest.

00:05:55.168 --> 00:05:57.980
- So I'm in complete agreement with you

00:05:57.980 --> 00:05:59.780
about the latter project.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:02.510
That that's certainly the one
that I've been engaged in.

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:05.660
I think one of the
things that happens when

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:08.760
you're doing the kind of

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.180
standard analytic metaphysics approach

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:14.820
is that you start with
a conceptual analysis,

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:19.820
with what causation looks like from

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:21.680
a conceptual point of view.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.590
And then you look in the world that

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:25.780
is the world as described by physics,

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:27.520
and you try to see
whether there's anything

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:29.660
that satisfies that description.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:32.360
And very often precisely
because the subject

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:34.840
and relationship between the subject

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:37.140
and the world has kind of gone out of you,

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.120
you don't find anything

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:41.870
that directly satisfies that description.

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:42.940
So I think it's not only

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:44.770
that there are these two
alternative projects,

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:48.210
but there's a way in
which the former project

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:49.920
is kind of bound to fail,

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.340
that comes into view when you're doing

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:53.913
the latter kind of project.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:57.770
The part that I didn't talk about it,

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:00.893
the part where you're
treating it as a normative,

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:04.700
you're looking for sort of
normative guidance about things

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:09.080
like causal search and
inference strategies.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:12.204
That's not something that
I talked about at all.

00:07:12.204 --> 00:07:14.300
It is something that you talk a lot about

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.801
in your new book, which I.

00:07:16.801 --> 00:07:19.120
And I wonder if you don't mind just taking

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:21.210
a couple of minutes to talk about that,

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:22.830
since the book isn't out.

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:25.000
And I've had a peek at some of it.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.530
I think people would be really interested

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:28.923
to hear sort of something about that.

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:31.970
- Okay. Well, thank you.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:36.423
So the book is called
"Causation with a Human Face:

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:41.020
Normative Theory and
Descriptive Psychology."

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:46.020
And it's forthcoming with
Oxford in the middle of October.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:49.690
And so what I try to do in the book,

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:54.120
it's a mixture of a lot
of different things,

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:57.940
but one of the central
projects or themes is to try

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:00.560
to bring together what I think of

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:05.220
as normative work about
causation and causal reasoning.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:06.950
And I see this going on,

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:07.810
as I said earlier,

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:11.220
in statistics, in machine learning,

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:16.130
machine discovery of causal relationships.

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:18.650
And I also actually think a fair amount

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:20.760
of the philosophical work that's been done

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:22.573
on causation is really,

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:27.040
the best way to understand
it is as normative proposals

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:28.410
of various kinds.

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:31.040
So there's a certain tendency, I think,

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:32.650
particularly in analytic metaphysics,

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:34.130
to think that the

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:37.840
project is somehow some
kind of descriptive one,

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:40.410
purely descriptive one that
is a description either

00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:42.160
of our concept of causation

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:43.920
or maybe a description of,

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:46.780
let's say like people in
this tradition like to say,

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:49.950
causal reality or what causation is.

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:53.070
And I think putting things that way faces

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:56.120
what in fact is a kind
of normative component.

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:57.980
The normative component being

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:00.570
that these are actually
proposals about how you want

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:02.470
to reason, think about causation.

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:04.500
They're not just purely descriptive.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:07.690
So anyway, there's this
normative component

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:10.640
that comes out of the disciplines
that I just described.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:15.640
And then there's a huge
burgeoning amount of work going on

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:21.600
in cognitive psychology,
developmental psychology,

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:25.823
to some extent in the
animal learning literature.

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:31.200
There's work in primatology,
et cetera, et cetera,

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.120
which is, and to some
extent also neurobiology,

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:37.270
which is descriptive of the sorts

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:41.190
of things that go on

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:43.573
that when people engage
in causal reasoning,

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:46.730
the kinds of causal
judgements people make,

00:09:46.730 --> 00:09:51.730
the procedures, cognitive and otherwise,

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:54.800
that they go through when they

00:09:56.748 --> 00:09:57.880
make causal judgements.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:00.790
And one of the things that
I try to do in the book

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:03.930
is to bring these two
literatures together.

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:05.890
And one of the ways in
which I think they can

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:08.080
be brought together is

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:12.560
if you're willing to adopt
what might be described

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:17.560
as a kind of rational actor
model of causal cognition.

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:20.477
And the basic idea here,

00:10:20.477 --> 00:10:23.100
and this fits with the general theme

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:26.520
that causation is this functional notion

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:30.140
that we use all the
time in our daily lives.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:32.613
The idea is that people,

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:35.740
when they engage in causal reasoning,

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:37.940
are often successful.

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:42.047
They do things in, at
least to some extent,

00:10:42.047 --> 00:10:45.780
and in some respects, are
normatively appropriate.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:50.780
So you can then connect the
normative and the descriptive

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:53.830
by asking such questions as, well,

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:56.720
a normative theory says that
the appropriate way to reason

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:59.170
in such and such a situation is so and so,

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:01.610
let's go look and see
whether people actually

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:03.310
in fact reason in that way.

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:05.240
And a whole lot of psychology experiments

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:06.980
have exactly this form.

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:09.640
And you do see a certain amount of

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:13.790
rationality or normatively
appropriate behavior

00:11:13.790 --> 00:11:17.550
in people's causal reasoning.

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:19.940
You can also go in the other direction.

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:24.090
If you find people doing
something regularly

00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:26.190
in their causal cognition,

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:29.100
and you have a theory that suggests that

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:32.110
it's either completely
normatively inappropriate

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:34.270
or maybe your theory says
nothing at all about whether

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:36.270
it's normatively appropriate at all,

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:39.290
then that can provide an impetus
to ask the question, well,

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:42.370
maybe we should rethink our
normative theory a little bit.

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:45.530
Maybe we're missing something

00:11:48.153 --> 00:11:48.986
in our normative accounts.

00:11:48.986 --> 00:11:52.910
So I see these kind of back
and forth interactions going on

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:57.630
between the normative and the descriptive.

00:11:57.630 --> 00:11:59.240
And so I try to emphasize in the book,

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:01.180
this isn't a matter of

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:05.330
deriving a not from an
is or anything like that.

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:07.640
It's rather that if you're willing

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:09.640
to make appropriate
additional assumptions,

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:12.300
like for example, the
rational actor assumption,

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:16.100
then you can connect the
normative and descriptive.

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:19.010
So that is one of the themes in the book.

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:23.280
And obviously my focus on the
psychology of causal inference

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:28.240
is very much in the spirit of
kind of putting the subject

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:32.030
or the human agent back into the picture,

00:12:32.030 --> 00:12:34.500
that the focus is on,

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:37.340
at least in part, on what we try to do

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:38.440
with causal reasoning.

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:43.660
What are the goals?
What are the functions?

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:45.510
What is it we're trying to accomplish

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:49.340
when we engage in causal reasoning?

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:53.060
So that's one theme in the book.

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:55.930
Another theme in the
book, just very quickly,

00:12:55.930 --> 00:13:00.930
is a certain skepticism about the tendency

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:04.740
in at least a lot of
contemporary analytic philosophy

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:06.500
to make a very sharp distinction

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:09.600
between metaphysics and epistemology.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:11.480
You certainly see this to some extent

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:15.270
in the analytic metaphysics
literature on causation,

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:16.870
and indeed on a lot of other topics,

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:20.560
where the idea is that what causation is

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:23.210
should be understood in some
way that it's totally divorced

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:25.480
from how we might find out about it.

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:29.880
And this, for reasons that I,
you can perhaps guess that,

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:31.650
I won't try to go into them in detail.

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:32.920
Now that strikes me as

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:38.713
not a very fruitful way of
approaching the subject.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:50.060
Okay, so another theme in
your discussion yesterday,

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:55.860
Jenann was what might be called

00:13:57.159 --> 00:14:01.300
the worldly underpinnings or
the physical infrastructure

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:04.650
that underlies causal reasoning.

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:07.510
And I take it you emphasize in particular

00:14:08.630 --> 00:14:11.110
the role of something like

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:16.330
thermodynamic gradients, and so on,

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:20.650
and the way in which
human agents are situated

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:25.613
in time and in this entropy gradient.

00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:31.140
Could you say a little
bit more about that,

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:34.240
and maybe also just
about the whole project

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:39.170
of talking about physical underpinnings?

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:42.410
I myself have some thoughts about that,

00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:44.550
and I'm actually working on a paper

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:48.143
with some other people
in connection with that.

00:14:49.243 --> 00:14:50.590
And I think it's an interesting project,

00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:51.980
and it's obviously, again,

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:54.790
rather different from asking, well,

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:58.630
what belongs to the concept
of causation and what doesn't?

00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:00.470
It's more like,

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:03.190
what are the preconditions
that have to be present

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:06.390
in the world for causal reasoning

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:08.333
to be possible and successful?

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:11.670
- Right, yeah, so the way, I mean,

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:13.410
the way it sort of comes
up in the philosophy

00:15:13.410 --> 00:15:16.580
of physics literature is
that for some time just

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:21.210
in the course of doing physics,

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:22.560
it became clear that there were

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:25.030
these high-level generalizations captured

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:29.350
in thermodynamics that had to be,

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:30.710
in some way, reconcile.

00:15:30.710 --> 00:15:32.930
That because they
incorporated it temporarily,

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:35.820
symmetry had to be in some way reconciled

00:15:35.820 --> 00:15:38.910
with the underlying time
symmetric physical laws.

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:41.170
And what it means to say
that the underlying laws

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:42.060
are time symmetric,

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:45.500
it means that for any
physically possible trajectory

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:50.500
that goes from a state at
time T1 to a state at time T2,

00:15:52.490 --> 00:15:55.810
there's a physically possible
reverse of that trajectory.

00:15:55.810 --> 00:16:00.163
So one that goes from state S2 at T2,

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:02.070
sorry, from S2,

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:04.110
which is the final state in the original,

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:07.070
to the initial state in the original.

00:16:07.070 --> 00:16:08.500
But of course we look around us

00:16:08.500 --> 00:16:13.090
and we see all sorts of
temporarily asymmetric processes.

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:16.720
It became sort of widely accepted that all

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:19.050
of the temporarily asymmetric processes

00:16:19.050 --> 00:16:22.490
that we actually see are
the ones that are captured

00:16:22.490 --> 00:16:25.033
by the second law of thermodynamics.

00:16:27.181 --> 00:16:29.570
There's might be some
wiggle room about that,

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:31.790
but they capture by far the largest body

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:35.580
of, say, temporarily
asymmetric physical phenomena

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:36.413
in the world.

00:16:36.413 --> 00:16:38.940
So the project of
reconciling the second law

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:42.240
of thermodynamics, which are
time symmetric underlying laws,

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:46.240
was for a long time just a
straightforward physics project.

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:47.590
One that took a long time.

00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:49.550
There were a lot of kind of missteps

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:51.460
in the early history of thermodynamics,

00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:54.870
but probably in the last 50 or 60 years,

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:59.590
it really started to come into focus.

00:16:59.590 --> 00:17:02.810
Okay, now when we look at
agents and in particular,

00:17:02.810 --> 00:17:06.020
the first person kind of
perspective on the world

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:08.260
from the point of view
of an embedded agent,

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:12.220
we find two very striking
asymmetries that are kind

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:15.020
of pervasive from a
first-person point of view.

00:17:15.020 --> 00:17:16.577
One is the asymmetry of knowledge,

00:17:16.577 --> 00:17:19.000
and the other is a causal asymmetry.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:21.380
They asymmetry of
knowledge is this idea that

00:17:22.930 --> 00:17:25.710
we remember the (mic thuds),
sorry, we remember the past.

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:26.990
We don't remember the future.

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:30.880
We know more about the past we
than we do about the future.

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:32.440
It takes some time to actually give

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:34.730
a precise characterization of these,

00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:36.810
but take that as a rough and ready go.

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:40.050
The other one is obviously
the causal asymmetry,

00:17:40.050 --> 00:17:41.960
the idea that we think that from

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:43.680
a first-person point of view,

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:46.500
we think that we can affect
the future by acting some way

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:49.020
in the here and now, but not the past.

00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:51.353
And the question arose quite naturally,

00:17:54.080 --> 00:17:56.080
are those in some way connected

00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:58.130
to the asymmetry of thermodynamics?

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:01.523
- Great, but, a quick
question though, Jenann.

00:18:03.520 --> 00:18:05.727
There's a fundamental
difference between the past

00:18:05.727 --> 00:18:10.033
and the present in terms of
access to information, right?

00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:15.320
That you cannot circumvent
no matter what, right?

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:18.450
And that's of course tied to
the directionality of time,

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:19.550
and so on.

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:21.850
But, I mean, is it useful to see it as

00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:25.630
about being about information
rather than entropy,

00:18:25.630 --> 00:18:26.960
or are they interchangeable?

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:28.223
I'm just curious.

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:31.650
- So, I mean,

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:34.470
these were the questions that
people were asking, right?

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:39.470
So I think the most illuminating
way to understand it

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:42.110
is to think in terms

00:18:43.250 --> 00:18:48.250
of what kind of information is
there available in the world

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:52.210
for an agent of the kind that has access

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:55.530
to its local environment to use?

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:57.160
If you ask the question that way,

00:18:57.160 --> 00:19:00.130
then you do get a natural answer.

00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:02.890
And the natural answer is
something like this, right?

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:05.230
So if you're just looking
at the macroscopic structure

00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:06.063
of the world, I mean,

00:19:06.063 --> 00:19:08.930
this is the one I went over
very quickly during the talk,

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:11.530
but the slightly more
detailed answer is this.

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:14.620
If you're looking at the
macroscopic structure of the world,

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:17.900
and you assume that an
agent only has sort of,

00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:20.570
whatever you think direct access is,

00:19:20.570 --> 00:19:24.030
if you think there's some
sort of direct perceptual,

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:26.850
that is there some basic
notion of information

00:19:27.691 --> 00:19:31.090
that comes from some sort of
causal channel with the world

00:19:32.370 --> 00:19:33.300
on the part of an agent,

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:35.110
those are the perceptual channels.

00:19:35.110 --> 00:19:38.470
If you take information that's
coming through that channel

00:19:38.470 --> 00:19:39.760
and you ask yourself,

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:43.930
what can an agent, in
a generic physically,

00:19:43.930 --> 00:19:46.530
a generic world that
obeys the Newtonian laws,

00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:48.770
what can an agent like
that know about the rest

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:49.603
of the world?

00:19:49.603 --> 00:19:51.950
What can an agent that has that kind of

00:19:51.950 --> 00:19:56.420
contemporary causal contact
with its own local environment

00:19:56.420 --> 00:19:59.830
parlay into in terms of knowledge
of the rest of the world?

00:19:59.830 --> 00:20:02.820
Assume just to be, I mean,

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:05.190
to make it a kind of
in principle question,

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:07.680
assume it knows the physical laws.

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:09.400
And ask what can it infer

00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:11.720
from local information
about its environment?

00:20:11.720 --> 00:20:12.723
So you say,

00:20:13.590 --> 00:20:17.640
what can it infer as a matter
of physical, almost nothing.

00:20:17.640 --> 00:20:19.940
Everything that it can infer
depends on what's going on

00:20:19.940 --> 00:20:20.790
in other places.

00:20:20.790 --> 00:20:21.873
It depends on,

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:26.110
we know as a matter of physical
law that the total state

00:20:26.110 --> 00:20:27.720
of the world at one time,

00:20:27.720 --> 00:20:29.700
if it's known,

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:31.930
can allow an agent that
knows the physical laws,

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:34.370
to know everything about
the past and future,

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:37.510
but we also know that
anything less than that,

00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:40.223
it can know almost nothing, right?

00:20:42.210 --> 00:20:45.040
But now if you take

00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:47.473
the statistical postulate
of thermodynamics,

00:20:48.620 --> 00:20:51.100
the ordinary canonical
probability distribution

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:53.140
that the world is in a given micro state,

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:56.590
given its macro state, and
you add that, and now you ask,

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:58.970
well, what can it know from
the physical laws together

00:20:58.970 --> 00:21:00.800
with probability distribution?

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:02.600
Again, the answer is almost nothing.

00:21:02.600 --> 00:21:04.260
If you think about it
carefully, you'll say,

00:21:04.260 --> 00:21:05.270
well, of course.

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:07.010
That's because if I'm just looking

00:21:07.010 --> 00:21:09.270
at my local environment here

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:11.380
and I'm asking what's gonna happen a day

00:21:11.380 --> 00:21:12.600
or a month from now,

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:15.220
everything depends on
the exogenous influences

00:21:15.220 --> 00:21:17.850
that are gonna impact
between now and then.

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:19.270
I mean, literally everything.

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:21.560
You can take any state,

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:24.610
at any finite fraction of,

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:27.410
or take any event or any finite fraction

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:28.830
of an interval into the future,

00:21:28.830 --> 00:21:31.910
you can find a solution
to the physical laws

00:21:31.910 --> 00:21:34.780
in which that event happens
and that event doesn't

00:21:34.780 --> 00:21:38.100
just by putting the right
exogenous influences in.

00:21:38.100 --> 00:21:39.860
Same in the backward direction.

00:21:39.860 --> 00:21:41.310
And you know it's gonna be the same

00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:42.770
in the backward direction,

00:21:42.770 --> 00:21:44.570
because the laws are time symmetric.

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:50.090
But if you add a low
entropy initial state,

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:53.590
a low entropy state sometime
in the distant past, right?

00:21:53.590 --> 00:21:55.163
Things change completely.

00:21:57.700 --> 00:21:59.200
Now an agent that's situated,

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:01.870
looking at its local
macroscopic environment,

00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:04.460
who has access only to
the macroscopic states

00:22:04.460 --> 00:22:05.727
of things around it,

00:22:05.727 --> 00:22:07.840
can know a whole lot about the past.

00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:08.673
Why is that?

00:22:08.673 --> 00:22:13.200
Because for any semi-ordered
state of its environment,

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:15.620
given a low entropy past,

00:22:15.620 --> 00:22:18.250
a low entropy state in the distant past,

00:22:18.250 --> 00:22:21.790
it can infer a lot about
what happened between

00:22:21.790 --> 00:22:25.603
that distant past state and
the state of the system now.

00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:30.250
That's the physical
underpinnings of what's going on.

00:22:30.250 --> 00:22:31.750
When you look at a state like

00:22:34.310 --> 00:22:39.310
a scar in a tree trunk or
a footprint in the sand or

00:22:42.510 --> 00:22:46.130
a half melted ice cube
in a glass of water.

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:48.360
What you're doing is
effectively you're inferring

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:50.350
from its current state and knowledge

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:52.290
that it must be decaying from

00:22:52.290 --> 00:22:55.610
some lower entropy state in the past,

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:58.440
that at some point somebody put,

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:01.610
it was in a more ordered
state than it is right now.

00:23:01.610 --> 00:23:04.510
So that's what's really going on

00:23:04.510 --> 00:23:07.200
in connecting sort of
the entropic gradient

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:09.690
with the kind of
information that's available

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:11.960
to an agent to use and

00:23:11.960 --> 00:23:14.420
to kind of parlay into
information about what's going on

00:23:14.420 --> 00:23:15.530
at other times.

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:19.950
Patently time asymmetric
has more to do now,

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:22.340
I mean, that's the step

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:25.350
that lets you go from
something about entropy

00:23:25.350 --> 00:23:27.733
to something about
knowledge or information.

00:23:29.200 --> 00:23:31.050
Did that answer your question, Priya?

00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:34.063
- Yeah. Thank you, yeah.

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:37.723
- Now I've forgotten what the thread was

00:23:37.723 --> 00:23:39.530
that we were talking about.

00:23:39.530 --> 00:23:41.133
- Well, in general,

00:23:42.033 --> 00:23:44.403
the worldly underpinnings of-

00:23:45.442 --> 00:23:46.747
- Oh, right. Okay-

00:23:46.747 --> 00:23:51.420
- Causal reasoning and inference, I would

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:58.100
wanna add some other possibilities

00:23:58.100 --> 00:24:00.760
that I think are maybe also part

00:24:00.760 --> 00:24:02.270
of the worldly underpinnings.

00:24:02.270 --> 00:24:05.590
And I don't myself understand very clearly

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:10.590
how these might be related
to the thermodynamic gradient

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:13.100
and entropy increase, and so on.

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:16.060
And perhaps you might help me with that.

00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:19.840
So one general feature

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:24.610
which seems to be present in our world,

00:24:24.610 --> 00:24:27.180
and is incorporated in a whole
lot of physical reasoning

00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:29.380
is some sort of notion of

00:24:30.810 --> 00:24:34.250
the effect of independence often enough

00:24:35.200 --> 00:24:40.000
of incoming influences, so to speak.

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:41.283
So we don't, for example,

00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:46.820
if you have an accelerating
electrical charge,

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:51.820
you imagine that it will radiate outwards

00:24:52.545 --> 00:24:56.780
with a spherical wave of
electromagnetic radiation.

00:24:56.780 --> 00:25:00.430
You don't imagine that
you're going to see such

00:25:00.430 --> 00:25:03.350
a wavefront coming in from a distance

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:06.620
and collapsing on itself into a point.

00:25:06.620 --> 00:25:08.250
And the idea is that,

00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:09.420
in the second case,

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:13.970
this requires some fantastic
level of coordination

00:25:13.970 --> 00:25:18.970
between what we think of as
independent causal influences

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:22.453
and that, at least in most cases,

00:25:24.695 --> 00:25:25.528
it doesn't happen in nature.

00:25:25.528 --> 00:25:28.040
Now in that particular case,

00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:31.760
I take it that there is
some sort of connection with

00:25:33.084 --> 00:25:35.590
the same kind of physical
reasoning that underpins

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:40.670
the second law of
thermodynamics, and so on,

00:25:40.670 --> 00:25:43.240
the kinds of assumptions
of Boltzmann made about

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:49.410
uncorrelated lists of molecular motions

00:25:49.410 --> 00:25:51.070
immediately prior to collision.

00:25:51.070 --> 00:25:53.890
So you can see the connection there.

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:56.490
But I think that's just
one of the things that

00:25:56.490 --> 00:25:59.080
is among these underpinnings
of physical reasoning.

00:25:59.080 --> 00:26:02.790
And a couple of other
things that strike me as

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:07.490
also present are the following.

00:26:07.490 --> 00:26:09.900
One is simply that a lot
of physical systems have

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:11.980
a certain kind of modularity to them

00:26:11.980 --> 00:26:15.770
in the sense that you don't really have

00:26:15.770 --> 00:26:18.260
to take into account everything (chuckles)

00:26:18.260 --> 00:26:21.770
in what's going on in
the external environment

00:26:21.770 --> 00:26:25.290
in understanding their behavior.

00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:28.020
And this is probably,

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:29.750
and probably the right
way to think about this

00:26:29.750 --> 00:26:30.750
is it's the kind of,

00:26:32.080 --> 00:26:34.466
in the physicist jargon,

00:26:34.466 --> 00:26:37.890
a kind of effective independence
or effective modularity.

00:26:37.890 --> 00:26:42.800
It isn't that the external
influences aren't there at all.

00:26:42.800 --> 00:26:47.020
It's just that they're very
small and they can be neglected,

00:26:47.020 --> 00:26:51.770
or maybe if they need to
be taken into account,

00:26:51.770 --> 00:26:55.210
they can be taken into
account just by talking about

00:26:55.210 --> 00:26:57.850
some very small number of variables

00:26:57.850 --> 00:27:00.370
and parameters that are on the,

00:27:00.370 --> 00:27:03.090
you think of as on the
boundary of the system.

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:04.380
You don't have to model everything

00:27:04.380 --> 00:27:06.540
in the environment in
detail, in other words,

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:08.920
to get the behavior of the system.

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:11.140
And it just seems that a
lot of physical systems

00:27:11.140 --> 00:27:13.060
in our world are like that.

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:16.980
Another notion that
strikes me as important

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:20.230
is the presence of what I

00:27:21.386 --> 00:27:23.560
have called in a couple of papers,

00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:26.550
approximate realization independence

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:30.840
or approximate conditional
causal independence,

00:27:30.840 --> 00:27:35.740
where the idea is that at
least for many systems,

00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:39.580
you don't have to know the details

00:27:39.580 --> 00:27:42.520
of the physics all the way down

00:27:42.520 --> 00:27:46.230
to understand their macro behavior.

00:27:46.230 --> 00:27:51.230
So Leo Kandinov and Nigel Goldenfeld

00:27:52.060 --> 00:27:56.490
give the example of modeling
the behavior of a bulldozer.

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:59.360
And they say, well,
you don't need to bring

00:27:59.360 --> 00:28:03.460
in quantum chromodynamics
to understand the behavior

00:28:03.460 --> 00:28:04.493
of a bulldozer.

00:28:05.330 --> 00:28:06.230
You can model it

00:28:09.124 --> 00:28:09.957
at a much higher level.

00:28:09.957 --> 00:28:13.010
Now you could imagine an
alternative world in which

00:28:13.010 --> 00:28:14.750
to model anything at all,

00:28:14.750 --> 00:28:17.560
you had to understand
the physics at the very,

00:28:17.560 --> 00:28:19.240
very deepest level.

00:28:19.240 --> 00:28:20.530
And of course that would be a world

00:28:20.530 --> 00:28:23.610
in which would be very
difficult to do science

00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:27.340
or learn about relationships in nature.

00:28:27.340 --> 00:28:29.220
But our world doesn't
seem to be like that.

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:30.850
There's a kind of,

00:28:30.850 --> 00:28:35.850
a proximate decoupling often
between different scales.

00:28:37.350 --> 00:28:40.570
And I think that's also something

00:28:40.570 --> 00:28:43.920
that facilitates causal
reason in our world.

00:28:43.920 --> 00:28:48.090
And I'm not sure exactly
how these are related

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:49.260
to the second law.

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:52.570
It isn't obvious to me that
they all just sort of fall out

00:28:52.570 --> 00:28:54.439
of the second law.

00:28:54.439 --> 00:28:56.563
And I wonder if you have
some thoughts about that.

00:28:57.450 --> 00:28:59.550
- You see, there there's a lot there.

00:28:59.550 --> 00:29:00.650
So first on

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:06.200
the microscopic sort of
chaos and macroscopic order.

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:08.587
So that's the full statement actually

00:29:08.587 --> 00:29:10.310
of the past hypothesis.

00:29:10.310 --> 00:29:13.010
So you assume that it's a
low entropy initial state,

00:29:13.010 --> 00:29:16.050
so there's macroscopic
order, but other than that,

00:29:16.050 --> 00:29:18.563
you assume uncorrelated micro condition

00:29:21.506 --> 00:29:22.339
- Okay.

00:29:23.230 --> 00:29:26.730
- On the modularity, so
this is an interesting-

00:29:26.730 --> 00:29:29.200
- But can I just stop you for a second.

00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:32.600
In connection with the past
hypothesis, and I have to admit,

00:29:32.600 --> 00:29:37.330
I've never quite understood
the ins and out of that.

00:29:37.330 --> 00:29:40.420
If you were just modeling the behavior

00:29:40.420 --> 00:29:41.930
of a gas or something,

00:29:41.930 --> 00:29:43.060
that it's a non-equilibrium state,

00:29:43.060 --> 00:29:45.270
and it's going to equilibrium,

00:29:45.270 --> 00:29:46.430
it doesn't seem like you would have

00:29:46.430 --> 00:29:50.260
to bring the past type hypothesis
directly into the picture.

00:29:50.260 --> 00:29:52.260
All you have to do is to talk about

00:29:55.590 --> 00:29:59.390
the state of the gas at the moment.

00:29:59.390 --> 00:30:00.490
And then you make

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:03.740
assumptions about, or
inquired ladenness or

00:30:05.010 --> 00:30:07.440
equal probability over
all the micro states,

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:09.840
consistent with the macro
state and so on and so forth.

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:13.620
- So that's inferring its
future and inferring its past,

00:30:13.620 --> 00:30:15.420
you need the past hypothesis.

00:30:15.420 --> 00:30:16.253
- Okay. Okay.

00:30:16.253 --> 00:30:19.150
- So if you just think of
it in time symmetric terms,

00:30:19.150 --> 00:30:20.910
because the laws are time symmetric,

00:30:20.910 --> 00:30:23.800
probability postulate is time symmetric.

00:30:23.800 --> 00:30:26.700
So if you take those things
and evolve them forward,

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:28.900
you're gonna get exactly
the same results as

00:30:28.900 --> 00:30:31.360
if you take those things
and evolve them backwards.

00:30:31.360 --> 00:30:34.810
So if you didn't have the past hypothesis,

00:30:34.810 --> 00:30:37.990
you ought to infer, not only the system,

00:30:37.990 --> 00:30:39.100
if it's a gas with

00:30:40.736 --> 00:30:42.660
partly collected in one corner,

00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:45.350
you have to ensure not only
is the system just running

00:30:45.350 --> 00:30:47.350
the dynamical laws forward,

00:30:47.350 --> 00:30:50.750
gonna go to an increasingly
dispersed state.

00:30:50.750 --> 00:30:52.610
You have to, inferring backward,

00:30:52.610 --> 00:30:55.770
assume that's also coming
from a dispersed state.

00:30:55.770 --> 00:30:59.820
So that in fact is collapsing
into a lower entropy stage,

00:30:59.820 --> 00:31:01.480
just a chance fluctuation,

00:31:01.480 --> 00:31:03.530
the probability distribution yields that.

00:31:04.400 --> 00:31:07.300
So the past hypothesis
is an hypothesis that

00:31:07.300 --> 00:31:08.650
when you're inferring back,

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:11.813
when you're kind of retrodicting
rather than predicting,

00:31:12.810 --> 00:31:15.510
it's a brute postulate that
gets you the right state

00:31:15.510 --> 00:31:16.921
in the distant past.

00:31:16.921 --> 00:31:18.420
- Right.

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:19.790
- I'll just say one
more thing that connects

00:31:19.790 --> 00:31:21.630
with our previous,

00:31:21.630 --> 00:31:23.250
the talk about information.

00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:25.770
That's also needed to
get things like that,

00:31:25.770 --> 00:31:27.790
a semi-ordered state of the environment,

00:31:27.790 --> 00:31:31.760
is in fact a record of
a more ordered state

00:31:31.760 --> 00:31:32.820
in its distant past,

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:34.928
'cause otherwise the right thing to say

00:31:34.928 --> 00:31:37.210
when you see footprint in the sand

00:31:37.210 --> 00:31:39.720
is not that someone stepped there,

00:31:39.720 --> 00:31:42.890
but that in fact it was
a chance fluctuation from

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:47.330
a beach at higher entropy.

00:31:47.330 --> 00:31:48.213
That's the idea.

00:31:50.080 --> 00:31:52.920
- Yeah, I don't know to
what extent our audience

00:31:52.920 --> 00:31:55.570
is interested in the status
of the past hypothesis.

00:31:55.570 --> 00:31:58.640
And as I say, I'm certainly no expert,

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:01.070
but I have to say that one
of the things that I find

00:32:01.070 --> 00:32:05.140
