00:32:51.560 --> 00:32:56.520
So it sounds as though
we, at least in cosmology,

00:32:56.520 --> 00:32:57.710
and I'd actually be interested

00:32:57.710 --> 00:32:59.623
in what Priya thinks about this,

00:33:00.830 --> 00:33:04.920
we think that we have
information about the past

00:33:06.019 --> 00:33:09.300
of the early universe and
its low entropy state that

00:33:13.873 --> 00:33:15.730
it seems like we're assuming we have

00:33:15.730 --> 00:33:17.270
some kind of independent access

00:33:17.270 --> 00:33:22.270
to that that doesn't require
use of the past hypothesis.

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:24.780
- Can I say something about that?

00:33:24.780 --> 00:33:27.140
- Maybe I'm just confused,
but that's my reaction.

00:33:27.140 --> 00:33:29.250
- So I wouldn't put it in epistemic terms.

00:33:29.250 --> 00:33:32.220
That's certainly the way
that Albert presents it.

00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:34.010
This is something that we have to suppose

00:33:34.010 --> 00:33:36.280
in order for our records to make sense.

00:33:36.280 --> 00:33:38.040
I would say rather, look,

00:33:38.040 --> 00:33:40.940
we live in a world in
which records are possible,

00:33:40.940 --> 00:33:44.630
because the world started
out in a low entropy state.

00:33:44.630 --> 00:33:46.687
And all of the things
that you're talking about

00:33:46.687 --> 00:33:49.340
are inferences from the current state-

00:33:49.340 --> 00:33:51.810
- From the current state.
Yeah, I agree with Jenann.

00:33:51.810 --> 00:33:54.040
I think that would be the point of view

00:33:54.040 --> 00:33:55.593
that I would take too, yeah.

00:33:57.230 --> 00:33:59.580
- Okay, well that, I mean,
that is fine with me.

00:33:59.580 --> 00:34:03.030
That makes perfect sense
as far as I'm concerned.

00:34:03.030 --> 00:34:04.800
- So one way to see it is to say, look,

00:34:04.800 --> 00:34:07.550
if we found out that there
