00:37:47.280 --> 00:37:49.840
I was just wondering about whether we have

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:51.600
to assume anything as specific

00:37:51.600 --> 00:37:56.423
as the past hypothesis in
the David Albert sense.

00:37:58.023 --> 00:38:00.281
- It's not that specific actually.

00:38:00.281 --> 00:38:01.333
- Yeah, I think it's,

00:38:01.333 --> 00:38:03.990
I find it a little more sweeping

00:38:05.942 --> 00:38:07.313
the past hypothesis.

00:38:08.232 --> 00:38:11.110
And I know I agree with the Jenann's way

00:38:11.110 --> 00:38:14.543
of phrasing it rather than David's.

00:38:18.310 --> 00:38:21.780
But I think part of the, I've tried,

00:38:21.780 --> 00:38:25.220
one can think about it in sort
of a counterfactual, right?

00:38:25.220 --> 00:38:27.830
So what would have happened?

00:38:27.830 --> 00:38:30.290
I mean, the thing is you
immediately run around

00:38:30.290 --> 00:38:32.850
if you did not start from there.

00:38:32.850 --> 00:38:36.310
So the counterfactual
way is not effective way

00:38:36.310 --> 00:38:37.950
to understand this in this case, right?

00:38:37.950 --> 00:38:42.400
Because you very quickly
will reach the point of the,

00:38:42.400 --> 00:38:44.760
so we wouldn't be here
to ask this question if

00:38:44.760 --> 00:38:47.160
that, this, this, that
had not happened, right?

00:38:47.160 --> 00:38:49.290
So, yeah.

00:38:49.290 --> 00:38:52.120
- And the very theories that we use to

00:38:53.140 --> 00:38:55.980
sort of guide our sense of
those sorts of counterfactuals

00:38:55.980 --> 00:39:00.240
is itself based on evidence,

00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:02.940
inferences from which rests
on the past hypothesis.

00:39:02.940 --> 00:39:05.019
So everything would be
up for grabs and in-

00:39:05.019 --> 00:39:05.852
- Yeah, I was gonna say,

00:39:05.852 --> 00:39:08.570
then everything becomes,
up for grabs, right?

00:39:08.570 --> 00:39:13.430
So, yeah. It's a hard one.

00:39:13.430 --> 00:39:17.483
I mean, this is a really
difficult question so.

00:39:18.405 --> 00:39:21.450
- I mean, one way to ask it
would be, and this, I mean,

00:39:21.450 --> 00:39:24.160
this isn't at all implausible that

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:28.420
sort of our theoretical
knowledge progresses

00:39:30.090 --> 00:39:33.530
to a stage where we now
have a very specific idea

00:39:33.530 --> 00:39:37.000
of what kind of initial state
that the universe was in.

00:39:37.000 --> 00:39:39.690
I think what Albert is doing is saying

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:41.700
in order to underwrite

00:39:41.700 --> 00:39:43.880
the sorts of asymmetries that are embodied

00:39:43.880 --> 00:39:45.310
in our causal reasoning,

00:39:45.310 --> 00:39:47.550
in our inferences to
the past is something,

00:39:47.550 --> 00:39:50.730
we need something that
looks generically like

00:39:50.730 --> 00:39:54.500
a state of macroscopic order in the past

00:39:54.500 --> 00:39:58.050
or some sort of boundary
condition that will restrict

00:39:58.050 --> 00:40:00.370
the states in the past

00:40:00.370 --> 00:40:03.510
in such a way that those
inferences will be underwritten.

00:40:03.510 --> 00:40:06.180
- Yeah, you need a
curtailment of the number

00:40:06.180 --> 00:40:07.650
of possible states,

00:40:07.650 --> 00:40:09.853
initial states from which you could start.

00:40:11.410 --> 00:40:13.620
They could be a set. It
doesn't have to be unique.

00:40:13.620 --> 00:40:16.100
But, I mean, there could be a set of them,

00:40:16.100 --> 00:40:19.140
but I think I find it quite difficult

00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:21.820
to understand even how we could explore

00:40:21.820 --> 00:40:24.447
what that set could be,

00:40:24.447 --> 00:40:27.103
because you very quickly run into the,

00:40:29.520 --> 00:40:30.680
moving forward that

00:40:31.560 --> 00:40:33.340
we wouldn't be here to
question blah, blah, blah,

00:40:33.340 --> 00:40:35.940
for many of even those
other possibilities, right?

00:40:35.940 --> 00:40:39.580
So it's pretty hard to
delimit that set as well.

00:40:43.630 --> 00:40:44.933
- Does that help, Jim?

00:40:47.740 --> 00:40:48.573
- Yeah, I mean,

00:40:48.573 --> 00:40:50.920
I don't know whether we
wanna continue on this topic,

00:40:50.920 --> 00:40:53.800
but I guess one of the
things that strikes me,

00:40:53.800 --> 00:40:58.800
and I talk about this in
a paper on the Flagpole,

00:40:59.720 --> 00:41:01.850
which you may know about,

00:41:01.850 --> 00:41:04.780
is that if in, for example,

00:41:04.780 --> 00:41:08.090
recent work in machine learning on

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:10.300
learning causal direction.

00:41:10.300 --> 00:41:14.720
So the paradigm is something like this.

00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:17.073
You have two correlated variables,

00:41:18.350 --> 00:41:20.690
you have purely
observational data that shows

00:41:20.690 --> 00:41:21.993
that they're correlated.

00:41:23.030 --> 00:41:24.473
You can somehow exclude the possibility

00:41:24.473 --> 00:41:26.650
that they have a common cause,

00:41:26.650 --> 00:41:30.463
but you don't know whether
X causes Y or Y causes X.

00:41:32.520 --> 00:41:37.520
Even in that apparently
very unfavorable setting,

00:41:38.220 --> 00:41:42.610
there are in fact algorithms
and strategies that

00:41:42.610 --> 00:41:46.160
will allow you to infer causal direction

00:41:46.160 --> 00:41:49.340
with a fairly high level of reliability.

00:41:49.340 --> 00:41:54.340
And what these procedures do
is that they exploit the fact

00:41:55.070 --> 00:41:58.120
that in this kind of setup,

00:41:58.120 --> 00:42:02.580
there is almost, at least
in a whole lot of cases,

00:42:02.580 --> 00:42:05.400
it's going to be possible to find

00:42:05.400 --> 00:42:09.750
an additional variable, call it Z,

00:42:09.750 --> 00:42:14.440
which for example maybe
uncorrelated with X,

00:42:14.440 --> 00:42:19.440
but correlated with Y, or
alternatively uncorrelated with Y,

00:42:19.760 --> 00:42:21.810
but correlated with X.

00:42:21.810 --> 00:42:24.040
And then you can explain that fact

00:42:24.040 --> 00:42:26.590
to reach the correct conclusion
about causal direction.

00:42:26.590 --> 00:42:30.670
So the correct direction

00:42:30.670 --> 00:42:32.910
is the direction in which you have

00:42:35.100 --> 00:42:37.280
a collider type structure in which

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:41.130
the two uncorrelated variables are causes

00:42:45.936 --> 00:42:47.090
of the other variable.

00:42:47.090 --> 00:42:50.014
Now in that kind of setup,

00:42:50.014 --> 00:42:53.800
you're exploiting the fact
that there's a certain amount

00:42:53.800 --> 00:42:58.400
of uncorrelatedness or
exogeneity out there,

00:42:58.400 --> 00:43:00.060
but it's all very local.

00:43:00.060 --> 00:43:02.560
You're not bringing,

00:43:02.560 --> 00:43:04.510
now maybe at the end of the day,

00:43:04.510 --> 00:43:07.330
it all depends on the past
hypothesis, I don't know,

00:43:07.330 --> 00:43:12.330
but you're certainly not
kind of directly making use

00:43:12.650 --> 00:43:15.420
of anything like the past hypothesis.

00:43:15.420 --> 00:43:17.640
You're making use of stuff that is very,

00:43:17.640 --> 00:43:21.040
as I say, very, very local to the system

00:43:21.040 --> 00:43:25.353
that you are reasoning about.

00:43:26.810 --> 00:43:30.440
And I guess I just think
it's of interest to try

00:43:30.440 --> 00:43:33.910
to understand how those
inferences work and

00:43:33.910 --> 00:43:36.530
what their warrant is and
what they assume about

00:43:36.530 --> 00:43:39.210
what's true of the systems,

00:43:39.210 --> 00:43:42.923
that you're using this
methodology to investigate.

00:43:44.390 --> 00:43:45.370
And you can do all that,

00:43:45.370 --> 00:43:48.210
I think, without necessarily bringing

00:43:48.210 --> 00:43:49.727
